", "lp": -0.12195564806461334, "top": [[" Arc", -0.12195564806461334], [" GPU", -2.8990724086761475]]}, {"t": " GPU", "lp": -0.3123571574687958, "top": [[" GPU", -0.3123571574687958], [" Linux", -1.7383825778961182]]}, {"t": " documentation", "lp": -0.28545042872428894, "top": [[" documentation", -0.28545042872428894], [" driver", -1.4876770973205566]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.025309164077043533, "top": [["\n\n", -0.025309164077043533], ["\n", -5.05468225479126]]}, {"t": "Would", "lp": -0.008029063232243061, "top": [["Would", -0.008029063232243061], ["Could", -5.914759635925293]]}, {"t": " you", "lp": -0.000995595008134842, "top": [[" you", -0.000995595008134842], [" like", -7.849390983581543]]}, {"t": " like", "lp": -0.001029305742122233, "top": [[" like", -0.001029305742122233], [" tell", -7.448489665985107]]}, {"t": " me", "lp": -0.013784809038043022, "top": [[" me", -0.013784809038043022], [" more", -4.464695453643799]]}, {"t": " to", "lp": -0.000688074273057282, "top": [[" to", -0.000688074273057282], [" elabor", -8.700555801391602]]}, {"t": " elaborate", "lp": -0.8696459531784058, "top": [[" elaborate", -0.8696459531784058], [" help", -0.9864519834518433]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another GPU context when your context needs one but none are available\n- More flexible but can cause **contention** between contexts\n- A CCS can be temporarily taken away from another running context\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: Each context is assigned its own dedicated CCS\n- More **predictable** behavior since contexts don't interfere with each other\n- If CCS resources are exhausted, allocation may fail rather than steal\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- This is typically the **most restrictive/dedicated** mode\n- Used when you want **guaranteed isolation** between contexts\n- Useful in scenarios where you need deterministic behavior and can't afford context interference\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n| Mode | Use Case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | General use, maximum flexibility, best throughput when you have many contexts |\n| **2** | Balance between flexibility and isolation; good default for most workloads |\n| **4** | Performance-critical or latency-sensitive workloads where context isolation matters, or when debugging contention issues |\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm not entirely certain about the exact semantics of mode 4 versus mode 2, as these driver parameters can vary between driver versions and I may be conflating details. \n\nFor accurate, version-specific documentation, I'd recommend checking:\n- The kernel source (`drivers/gpu/drm/xe/`)\n- The `xe` driver documentation in the Linux kernel tree\n- Intel's official Arc GPU documentation\n\nWould you like me to elaborate"}